BT 1 0 0 1 57.6 500.4 Tm .113725 .141176 .129412 rg 15.5 TL /F3+0 10.5 Tf 15.5 TL (An organization can look decisive because one capable person is holding the whole problem together. That is the) Tj T* (visible strength. The hidden weakness is that everyone else has learned to route judgment through that person.) Tj T*  T* (No head works alone. Give each role a bounded judgment to own, then make the connections among those roles) Tj T* (explicit.) Tj T*  T* (The Bowtie Problem forms when work arrives from many directions, but the organization pushes the judgment) Tj T* (into one head. Decisions then flow back out through that same person. At the center, an Overloaded Decision) Tj T* (Maker \(ODM\) carries too many kinds of judgment and too much of the organization's cognitive load. This) Tj T* (concentration forms a knot.) Tj T*  T* (The pattern may once have made sense. Information was scarce, security required compartmentalization,) Tj T* (urgency was high, or other roles would not decide. The knot becomes a loss of grip when another role has the) Tj T* (authority and context to make the decision, yet the decision still returns to the knot. As demand accumulates at) Tj T* (the knot, the organization responds too late, responds poorly, or does not respond at all.) Tj T* ET
